stioning of why why does that happen  how does that happen what is it  and reading the literature will help  it's a key to all our success and we've  leveraged that knowledge to make  advances for example our nutrition  research benefited from a very  little published or expanded 1962 study  climate change breeding will be a  challenge that we all have to look at  and  move forward with  so this is the diversity we see just for  um  [Music]  leaf morphology so normal apple leaf  looks like the smaller  leaf on the stone but we have some that  look like rosemary leaves and you can  see that the leaves are actually growing  on the trunk which we've never seen to  that extent before leaf loping  can appear in one progeny and  have very little expression but be  in the  background of other progenies  we were able to  amp up the expression of red leaf color  and  these make wonderful summer scholars or  graduate student studies for flowers you  saw the  initial  single blossoms but this is the kind of  variation we can have in terms of color  number of blossoms  vibrancy  a lot of double flowers and we had a  summer scholar that determined that  some  [Music]  genotypes that are homozygous for double  flowers are in fact lethals  tree form again what's not to love about  this so you have trees that have the  kilometer habit  are segregating for dwarfs are  shaped like a wheel without pruning  uh weeping  we want branch angles that are about 90  degrees for greatest strength and this  is just a snapshot of some of the  diversity  and then  i talk about complexity never have two  guys name ben in your lab because it  makes life very interesting but ben  archesky  is someone that looked at elvis  prunifolia and looked at something i was  really intrigued about which was pale  green lethal a lot of breeders said we  don't see pale green lethal  i saw a lot of our progenies 25 of the  population would die due to this  lethality  and ben uh took it on as a side project  and did such a good job with it it  became a nefa-funded  doctoral  scholarship for him  we made the connection that vitamin k  was involved in pilgrim lethality  and i had googled a colleague that  contacted me and found out that he was  an expert in phylloquinone again  serendipity perhaps being prepared  perhaps but he was able to  provide us with the means to assay for  vitamin k and very small catalytions  don archevsky offered the other ben ben  gutierrez the leftover malice printfolia  seeds because it seemed that they were  segregating for traits that ben thought  was of interest  so ben gutierrez surveyed the usda apple  collection at geneva he looked at  segregation of fluoridazine dihydro  chalcones fluoridazine trilobitin and  cybolden which are important in glucose  metabolism but also  in their role as bitter blockers and  sweeteners  he conducted  gbs  she  sorry gps  on this progeny  and uh this progeny is segregating for  all sorts of amazing things um he  convinced me to to plant several more of  those progenies  and we'll continue to look at this role  ben is now the curator of the apple  journalism collection so this  collaboration will be long-lived  switch a little bit because i'm talking  a lot about a lot of different things  but what's intriguing about this is as a  breeder as a geneticist seeing your  varieties in the supermarket is  absolutely priceless  having someone right to tell you that  it's their favorite apple it's  absolutely um amazing  and these were two apples that we did  under a club system where only certain  growers but in this case all growers in  new york state had the option to apply  the last three varieties that we have  released are open releases so that  anyone  has the opportunity to enjoy them  so in the future look for a fully  russeted apple  it's one of the most amazing tasting  apples that i've had the um  the good fortune to try and to develop  um  and then this is an example of some of  the output from our  ornamental breeding which is columnar  and double flower that's my technician  kevin maloney who is  a wonderful resource to the program  so challenges we're going to have  invasives every program is going to have  invasives how devastating they are and  what we can do about them  will require  collaboration with entomologists but we  have  brown spar  brown  marmorated stink bug ambrosia beetle and  spotted lanternfly which  are the latest slew of  invasives  climate change we know that that's going  to be a challenge i told you about it in  the north we're battling  not only frosts but hail so that's why  we use hail nets  in  the washington oregon area they're  battling drought  as opposed to this recent  a meme about uh in new york in our rains  but um  the drought and the heat are causing  sunburn that sunburn becomes a secondary  avenue  so how are we gonna breed for something  that can have such extreme  manifestations  and we are still pursuing this  so future opportunities  we have new flavors new markets hard  cider fresh cut healthy foods new  flavors textures  nutrients some of these will be club  varieties and others will be open to the  public at large  i want to thank you for your attention  and for being the next generation to be  following in our footsteps i have never  regretted my career choice a special  thanks to the apple industry cornell  colleagues my collaborators our field  staff kevin maloney and former students  and summer scholars  if there's time i'd be glad to open it  up for any questions  i thank you again for your attention and  for  your interest in plant breeding  you

